egin{aligned}1 & 5n \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nfor all $n\\in N.$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=$ {{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-2}}={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is {{field-6}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-7}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}={{field-9}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-10}} & ={{field-11}}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & ={{field-12}}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{field-13}} \\\\  & ={{field-14}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5n \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nis true for all {{field-15}}." 13194139534295 17592186045417 true] [17592186418037 1072 #uuid "61f38221-2cec-5289-9323-193f490aea4a" 13194139534295 17592186045417 true] [17592186418037 1074 "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),P(2),P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5n \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5(1) \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5k \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k+1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5(k+1) \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving matrix identities using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to factor out one instance of the matrix, as follows: \n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k+1}={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k+1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5k \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5k+5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5(k+1) \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required. \n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5n \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nis true for all $n\\in N.$" 13194139534295 17592186045417 true] [17592186418038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418011 13194139534295 17592186045417 true] [17592186418038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418013 13194139534295 17592186045417 true] [17592186418038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418014 13194139534295 17592186045417 true] [17592186418038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418015 13194139534295 17592186045417 true] [17592186418038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418018 13194139534295 17592186045417 true] [17592186418038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418020 13194139534295 17592186045417 true] [17592186418038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418023 13194139534295 17592186045417 true] [17592186418038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418024 13194139534295 17592186045417 true] [17592186418038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418026 13194139534295 17592186045417 true] [17592186418038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418028 13194139534295 17592186045417 true] [17592186418038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418029 13194139534295 17592186045417 true] [17592186418038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418032 13194139534295 17592186045417 true] [17592186418038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418033 13194139534295 17592186045417 true] [17592186418038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418035 13194139534295 17592186045417 true] [17592186418038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418036 13194139534295 17592186045417 true] [17592186418038 1069 "q-183878" 13194139534295 17592186045417 true] [17592186418038 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534295 17592186045417 true] [17592186418038 1071 "Use mathematical induction to prove that\n${[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}{5}^{n} & {5}^{n}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nfor all $n\\in N.$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=$ {{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-2}}={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is {{field-6}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-7}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}={{field-9}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-10}} & ={{field-11}}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & ={{field-12}}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{field-13}} \\\\  & ={{field-14}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n${[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & {5}^{n}-1 \\\\ 0 & {5}^{n}\\end{aligned}]$\nis true for all {{field-15}}." 13194139534295 17592186045417 true] [17592186418038 1072 #uuid "d15324a3-fbaa-5825-b749-4eff694b4d05" 13194139534295 17592186045417 true] [17592186418038 1074 "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),P(2),P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}{5}^{n} & {5}^{n}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{5}^{1} & {5}^{1}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$ \n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}=[\\begin{aligned}{5}^{k} & {5}^{k}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k+1}=[\\begin{aligned}{5}^{k+1} & {5}^{k+1}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving matrix identities using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to factor out one instance of the matrix, as follows: \n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k+1}={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k+1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{5}^{k} & {5}^{k}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5({5}^{k}) & 4({5}^{k})+({5}^{k}-1) \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{5}^{k}\\cdot {5}^{1} & {5}^{k}\\cdot {5}^{1}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{5}^{k+1} & {5}^{k+1}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n${[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}{5}^{n} & {5}^{n}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nis true for all $n\\ge 1.$" 13194139534295 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-02905748-e2b4-561e-a566-ae0b9b15da1a" 17592186417878 "answer-0619107f-9c7a-5d5c-82ed-999a2d9ed313" 17592186417879 "answer-06909409-0842-5d6c-a119-25b13308d091" 17592186417880 "answer-084412ea-de9b-59da-8285-c782f4c8e0b2" 17592186417881 "answer-0a1ec3d8-9378-5556-9162-03ac21c23652" 17592186417882 "answer-0a8eef2e-3b46-53c3-8109-d29c29f17a3c" 17592186417883 "answer-0dc8b06e-c864-5dbf-a712-c415f35957f6" 17592186417884 "answer-10af1c4f-918c-5aeb-bfe6-2778c07645d4" 17592186417885 "answer-1216f1b7-5d06-5564-b19b-823fbde39b47" 17592186417886 "answer-138c5cca-afeb-529c-8f04-a242bbef4ff4" 17592186417887 "answer-14733c38-5531-5907-a4ab-fe1dd97e1712" 17592186417888 "answer-15763898-4872-51cb-b657-033337daec95" 17592186417889 "answer-1b90bff6-346f-5d40-862c-a32f0d2b13c5" 17592186417890 "answer-21db2a0e-b590-571d-bb69-1a100a2cd78c" 17592186417891 "answer-245a6c93-b6b5-568b-a5f4-623f20065350" 17592186417892 "answer-2a077c17-b773-5812-8e1a-3201372561f6" 175921864